<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Harare</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Salisbury, Rhodesia, Fort Salisbury</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Harare is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>. It began as Fort Salisbury, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-south-africa-company">British South Africa Company</a>'s Pioneer Column raised the flag in September 1890, and grew into the capital of Southern Rhodesia, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federation-of-rhodesia-and-nyasaland">Federation of Rhodesia and Nyasaland</a> and Rhodesia. It was renamed Harare in 1982, two years after independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harare.jpg" alt="Harare" width="320" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Baynham Goredema from Johannesburg, South Africa · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crowne_Plaza,_Harare.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 18 April 1980 the Republic of Zimbabwe became independent; Salisbury was renamed <b>Harare</b> two years later.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rhodesia">Rhodesia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 1980s Derek Hudson, conductor of the Bulawayo Philharmonic Orchestra, needed long negotiations with the authorities before he could give Zimbabwe's first performance of Beethoven's Ninth Symphony, and at Christmas 1994 the tune in a fundraising organ recital at a <b>Harare</b> church provoked angry protests.</blockquote><a href="/songs/rise-o-voices-of-rhodesia">Rise, O Voices of Rhodesia</a></li><li><blockquote>Its Pioneer Column marched into Mashonaland and raised the flag at <b>Fort Salisbury</b> in September 1890.</blockquote><a href="/state/company-rule-in-rhodesia">Company rule in Rhodesia</a></li><li><blockquote>Despite African opposition, Britain created the Federation of Rhodesia and Nyasaland, or Central African Federation, in 1953, with its capital at <b>Salisbury</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/federation-of-rhodesia-and-nyasaland">Federation of Rhodesia and Nyasaland</a></li><li><blockquote>With sovereign rights to tax, grant land and issue its own stamps and currency, the company built Beira into the main port for the landlocked Rhodesias, linked by the Beira railway, which reached Umtali in 1898 and <b>Salisbury</b> in 1899.</blockquote><a href="/state/mozambique-company">Mozambique Company</a></li><li><blockquote>On 1 October 1923 Britain annexed it as the self-governing Colony of Southern Rhodesia, with Charles Coghlan as its first premier and <b>Salisbury</b> as its capital, ending Company rule in Rhodesia.</blockquote><a href="/state/southern-rhodesia">Southern Rhodesia</a></li></ul></section>
